# Line-ending policy.
#
# The repository is stored with LF, which is what every blob already committed
# uses. Without this file a Windows checkout leaves CRLF in the working tree
# while HEAD holds LF, so `git status` reports every file in the repo as
# modified and a one-line change is invisible inside a whole-file diff.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` tells git to normalize to LF on the way IN (commit) and
# check out LF on the way OUT, on every platform. Editors on Windows handle LF
# fine; the two file types that genuinely need CRLF are listed below.
#
# No history rewrite and no renormalization pass were needed here: the
# committed blobs were already LF, so the clean filter makes the CRLF working
# tree match HEAD immediately. If a stray file ever does linger as modified
# with an empty diff, `git add --renormalize .` settles it.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ---- explicit text types (belt and braces for the auto-detector) ----------
*.c        text eol=lf
*.h        text eol=lf
*.cpp      text eol=lf
*.hpp      text eol=lf
*.pio      text eol=lf
*.py       text eol=lf
*.sh       text eol=lf
*.md       text eol=lf
*.txt      text eol=lf
*.cmake    text eol=lf
*.json     text eol=lf
*.yml      text eol=lf
*.yaml     text eol=lf
*.svg      text eol=lf
*.conf     text eol=lf
*.service  text eol=lf
CMakeLists.txt text eol=lf
LICENSE    text eol=lf
.gitignore text eol=lf
.gitmodules text eol=lf

# ---- files that must keep CRLF -------------------------------------------
# Windows batch interpreters can mis-parse LF-only scripts.
*.bat      text eol=crlf
*.cmd      text eol=crlf

# ---- binary: never touch, never diff as text -----------------------------
*.png      binary
*.jpg      binary
*.jpeg     binary
*.gif      binary
*.ico      binary
*.pdf      binary
*.uf2      binary
*.elf      binary
*.bin      binary
*.hex      binary
*.exe      binary
*.pdb      binary
*.obj      binary
*.o        binary
*.a        binary
*.lib      binary
*.ilk      binary
*.xlsx     binary
*.docx     binary
*.pptx     binary
